    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the greatest common factor (GCF) of 40 and 48, we will use the prime factorization method.\n\n**Step 1: Prime Factorization**\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorization of each number.\n\n- **Prime factorization of 40:**\n  \\[\n  40 \\div 2 = 20 \\\\\n  20 \\div 2 = 10 \\\\\n  10 \\div 2 = 5 \\\\\n  5 \\div 5 = 1\n  \\]\n  So, the prime factorization of 40 is:\n  \\[\n  40 = 2^3 \\times 5\n  \\]\n\n- **Prime factorization of 48:**\n  \\[\n  48 \\div 2 = 24 \\\\\n  24 \\div 2 = 12 \\\\\n  12 \\div 2 = 6 \\\\\n  6 \\div 2 = 3 \\\\\n  3 \\div 3 = 1\n  \\]\n  So, the prime factorization of 48 is:\n  \\[\n  48 = 2^4 \\times 3\n  \\]\n\n**Step 2: Identify the Common Factors**\n\nNext, we identify the common prime factors and their lowest powers in the prime factorizations of both numbers.\n\n- The prime factorization of 40 is \\(2^3 \\times 5\\).\n- The prime factorization of 48 is \\(2^4 \\times 3\\).\n\nThe common prime factors are 2, and the lowest power of 2 present in both factorizations is \\(2^3\\).\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the GCF**\n\nThe GCF is the product of the lowest powers of the common prime factors.\n\\[\n\\text{GCF} = 2^3 = 8\n\\]\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of 40 and 48 is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
